body:
Interesting... we did add the OGL-UK licenses to the license list, which appear related to this one but are clearly substantively different. But the OGL-UK-* set are are designed to be used by anyone as the Licensor, and as @MarkAtwood noted, OGL-Canada-2.0 is only usable as a license from the specific named licensor.

@burritojustice, the dejacode and okfn links you mentioned appear to be from tools talking about the license itself. Do you have examples of data sets or other content that have been released under this license (not necessarily using the identifier, but the license itself)?

If this is used for a substantial amount of content released in the wild, I could be in favor of adding it to the list, despite it being limited to a single licensor. From a quick read of the terms, the single named licensor is the only qualm I have about it meeting the [license inclusion principles](https://github.com/spdx/license-list-XML/blob/master/DOCS/license-inclusion-principles.md).
